Until #7618 is released, a prevented close can keep the popup state if the popup rejects <kbd>Escape</kbd> and outside interactions before it requests a close, so `onClose` doesn't need to prevent them:

```tsx
// TODO: Remove this workaround when
// https://github.com/ariakit/ariakit/issues/7616 is fixed.
<Ariakit.ComboboxPopover
  hideOnEscape={false}
  hideOnInteractOutside={false}
  onClose={(event) => event.preventDefault()}
>
  {/* items */}
</Ariakit.ComboboxPopover>
```

The same props work on `SelectPopover` and other popups based on `Dialog`. When the close is prevented only under a condition, pass the condition as a callback, for example `hideOnEscape={() => !hasUnsavedChanges}`.

This extends the earlier `hideOnEscape={false}` suggestion with these limitations:

- It covers only <kbd>Escape</kbd> and outside interactions. Other close requests still set `open` to `false` before `onClose` prevents them, so they still reset the popup state. For example, clicking the select button while the popup is open still makes the selected item active again. `DialogDismiss` and `store.hide()` also still reset it.
- `onClose` no longer runs for <kbd>Escape</kbd> and outside interactions, so move any side effect that it runs for them, such as showing a warning, into the `hideOnEscape` and `hideOnInteractOutside` callbacks.
- The popup no longer handles <kbd>Escape</kbd>, so the key press also reaches <kbd>Escape</kbd> handlers outside the popup, such as an ancestor `onKeyDown` or a `document` or `window` listener. Ariakit parent dialogs still ignore it.

The recording in #7618 shows the workaround in the reproduction sandbox.
